<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="diffeq-notes-3-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Method of Characteristics</title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Use the method of characteristics to find fundamental solutions to linear homogeneous ODEs. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <p>
        Consider the ODE <md> ay'' + by' + cy = 0. </md> We have seen that exponentials often work out for us, so assuming the solution has the form <m>y = e^{\lambda x} </m> would give <m> (a\lambda^2 + b \lambda + c)e^{\lambda x} = 0 </m>. Seeing that <m>e^{\lambda x} \neq 0 </m> for all <m> x </m>, our problem boils down to solving the quadratic equation <md> (a\lambda^2 + b \lambda + c) = 0. </md> 
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-characteristic-eqn">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <alert>characteristic equation</alert> for the ODE is when we exchange derivative order for algebraic degree. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Naturally, since we are solving a quadratic equation, we may have the following cases. 

        <ol> 
            <li><p> <alert> Distinct real roots: </alert> we get <m>y(x) = c_1 e^{\lambda_1 x} + c_2 e^{\lambda_2 x} </m> </p></li> 
            <li><p> <alert> Repeated real roots: </alert> we get <m> y(x) = c_1e^{\lambda x} + c_2 e^{\lambda x} </m>. </p></li>
            <li><p> <alert> Complex conjugate roots: </alert> we get <m>y(x) = e^{\text{Re}(\lambda) x} \left(c_1 \sin(\text{Im}(\lambda)x) + c_2 \cos(\text{Im}(\lambda)x) \right). </m> </p></li> 
        </ol>
    </p>

</section> 
